ies this had less to  do with governments adopting new  economic theories than it did with the  ability of rulers to fulfill their  people's material needs  consequently these policies were not  exactly conducive to free and open trade  at the same time  if there were any one organizing trend  in the political structures of european  states during the 17th and 18th  centuries it was the drive toward the  creation of absolutist monarchies  the movement toward absolutism was  motivated by ever increasing costs of  government and the thirst for new  revenue sources to support it that is  the immediate and rising fiscal  requirements of the centralized  bureaucracy  and the biggest discretionary expense  for governments than as today was the  cost of maintaining armies in navies  probably one of the greatest mechanisms  for british financial stability was the  creation of the bank of england in 1694.  the bank of england allowed the  government to raise secured loans  thereby enhancing the financial security  of the state in ways that many other  states could not until the 19th century  the bank of england was independent of  the monarchy it was a creation of  parliament not the king  divorced from the whims of the crown the  bank offered investors a sense of  security and stability that was  unmatched in other parts of europe  what is the creation of a state bank to  do with industrialization  well financial security and stability it  offered allowed great britain to solve  many of the problems of revenue that  plagued the emerging absolutist states  it allowed for the creation of the  world's largest and most powerful navy  so commerce could rely on the power of  the government to protect trade  the british government never played any  direct or indirect role for that matter  in the development of new industrial  sectors during the 18th century  but the state was able to enjoy  financial stability that proved to be  very advantageous for growth  the general trends that i've just  presented were not restricted to great  britain all over europe greater  agricultural specialization and  increases in agricultural production  were becoming commonplace  labor relations were also shifting as  serfdom declined and labor tended to  become more specialized and centralized  in cities  governments grew in size and tended  toward greater centralization which  spurred new institutions favorable to  commerce  but the unique circumstances that  existed in great britain by about the  middle of the 18th century  made the industrial revolution possible  all that remained was what we might call  the spark of capitalism that would give  rise to industrial production  by 1750 all the preconditions for  modernity were in place but the world  was still in a very traditional state  some spark was needed to push humans  across the next threshold of complexity  into the age of modernity  that spark both literal and metaphorical  was the burning of a substance laid down  by geological processes millions of  years ago a substance called coal  it was lit on a small and hitherto  relatively obscure island off the  northwestern edge of eurasia  britain had depended on wood for most of  its energy before industrialization but  by the 18th century it had begun to run  out of wood  so britain's turned to coal for heating  and cooking sinking mines deeper and  deeper until groundwater seeped in and  flooded the shafts  this was the context in which steam  engines were invented to more  efficiently pump water out of the mines  early in the 1700s thomas newcomen  designed a steam engine with a piston  pushed by steam but it was so  inefficient that it proved almost  useless  it was scottish inventor james watt who  built a much more efficient steam engine  it pumped larger quantities of water and  could also be applied to drive other  types of machinery  in the early 1800s watts steam engine  was attached to spinning and weaving  machines which made rapid cheap mass  production of textiles possible driving  down the price of cotton cloth by 80  by the middle of the 19th century  textiles had become the largest business  in britain employing half a million  people out of 12 million total  they had also destroyed the  comparatively inefficient cotton  industry in india with cheap  british exports the first sign of the  huge shift in global power that was  about to occur  it wasn't long before the iron and steel  industry was similarly transformed  using a technique pioneered by abraham  derby which involved smelting iron by  burning coke instead of charcoal british  iron production sword  later henry bessemer built a blast  furnace to produce an even stronger  version of iron steel although we  shouldn't forget that the chinese han  dynasty had managed something similar 2  000 years earlier  these developments in the coal-fired  steam engine and iron production led to  a dramatic expansion in railway and  maritime transportation  by the mid-19th century almost 13 000  miles of rail tracks had been laid  across britain and steam ships were  crossing the atlantic ocean this was a  veritable explosion of innovation a  revolution driven by collective learning  that allowed humans to soar past the  dying off point thomas malthus had  predicted  the british population and standard of  living reached unprecedented levels and  london became the largest city on earth  london hosted the first world's fair in  1851 showing off the fruits of british  innovation in a vast hall dubbed the  crystal palace itself a marvel of  structural engineering made of iron and  glass covering 19 acres  this damp little island had led the  world into the modern era but more than  that britain had established a model for  industrialization that could be imitated  by other nations in which similar  preconditions existed and in the second  wave belgium france prussia and the  united states all joined the club  before the industrial revolution  britain produced just two percent of the  world's industrial goods  by the middle of the 19th century that  percentage increased tenfold to a  staggering  20 percent  one-fifth of the world's industrial  output  still  the inequities that industrialization  could also produce  are best seen in the cotton industry  the growth in the production of cotton  together with the falling price of  textiles  allowed ordinary people in  industrialized nations to have access to  more textile goods than ever before  but  it also led to an acceleration of the  slave trade  because of the vast amount of money now  to be made in cotton  there was increased demand for slaves to  work the plantations of the americas  and the number of slaves forcibly  brought from africa to the americas each  year more than doubled after the  spinning jenny's debut  once the first steam ships made their  made in ocean voyages in 1819  the pace of transoceanic travel also  quickened  with travel faster and safer than it had  been in the age of sale power  steem power enabled international trade  to multiply  initially the supply of potential  workers exceeded factory owners needs  this created a potentially exploitative  situation  employers didn't have any reason to pay  what might be construed as living wages  the situation was exacerbated by the  fact that many factory jobs required  little to no skill  and unskilled workers stood near the  bottom of the labor hierarchy  so  most early industrial workers labored  long hours for low pay under arduous  circumstances  men women and children  worked under conditions that compromised  their health and safety  families often work together children  alongside their parents  as they had on the farms in prior  generations  and indeed  child labor was a necessity for most  poor families  the oxford historian jane humphries  places the number of child workers in  early 19th century britain at more than  a million  of these 350 000 were between the ages  of 7 and 10.  they constituted 15  of the british industrial workforce  were so many children employed  it wasn't just that they came to work  with their parents each day  factory owners viewed them as useful and  cheap  children performed jobs suited to their  small stature  they would stand at spinning machines  and repair breaks in the thread or crawl  underneath machinery to clean out  obstructions  still  many british people came to believe that  employing children in dangerous drudgery  was both exploitative and distasteful  the industrial revolution and rapid  urbanization  also led to a physical separation of the  various social classes in london and  elsewhere that hadn't previously existed  the middle and upper classes  increasingly avoided poorer areas of the  city  seeing them as enveloped in prostitution  drunkenness and crime  factory owners and parliamentarians  viewed these social ills as indicative  of the poor's inherently corrupt nature  and not as signs of being underpaid nor  owing to a lack of public services and  protections  crowded cities also meant poor  ventilation growing piles of rubbish and  waste and contaminated water that  brought typhoid and diarrhea  dark damp overcrowded apartments  facilitated the spread of such airborne  illnesses as influenza pneumonia and  tuberculosis  hundreds of poor neighbors would queue  up before a single outdoor water tap for  drinking water  bass and cleaning  the water would be turned on only a few  hours a week  and you might wait hours to fill your  buckets  private toilets didn't exist in poorer  sections of town  this led to filthy conditions a loss of  dignity  and the spread of disease among tenement  residents  in 1849 the investigative journalist  henry mayhew visited a neighborhood in  southeast london known as burmanzee  where thousands of residents died of  cholera that year in this part of town  the poor relied upon drinking water  contaminated by human waste and rotting  animal carcasses  mayhew described the area as  a foul stagnant ditch  charles dickens  captured such blight in his serialized  novel oliver twist  describing the slums of south london  dickens wrote of rooms  so small  so filthy  so confined  that the air would seem too tainted  even for the dirt and squalor which they  shelter  but the most influential critique of  industrial society came from the german  philosophers karl marx and friedrich  engels  engels had worked in a textile factory  in manchester and eviscerated the  conditions he found there in his 1844  book the condition of the working class  in england  four years later he and marx published  the communist manifesto in which they  offered a comprehensive theory of human  history in which the main driver of  change was class conflict  because the gap between rich and poor  would continue growing exponentially in  this capitalist environment they  predicted a revolution was inevitable it  would overthrow capitalism and replace  it with a communist system that would  more equitably share the fruits of  production  one day after the communist manifesto  appeared in 1848 demonstrations and  riots broke out in paris and two days  later the french king abdicated revolts  also erupted in the italian states the  habsburg empire in switzerland spain  denmark and britain and even the pope  was forced to flee were marx and engel's  predictions coming true  these revolutions of 1848 were driven by  poverty and the appalling conditions  created by industrialization but the  military power of the state was too  strong and monarchical authority was  restored  in the developing world the industrial  nations treated their colonies as  sources of cheap raw materials and  producers of cash crops they destroyed  landscapes forests entire ecosystems in  their extraction of gold silver and  diamonds and in their efforts to  increase harvests of coffee tea sugar  and rubber  european scientists were aware of the  devastating consequences of this british  east india company scientists reported  in 1852 on the probable global impacts  of the destruction of tropical forests  even mentioning potential climate change  animals also faced worldwide devastation  the furbearing mammals of the russian  steps and the american prairies were  pretty well exterminated by 1914 as were  huge numbers of birds and their habitats  some of this activity was purely  recreational but devastating nonetheless  in africa british hunters shot thousands  of antelope elephants giraffe and  rhinoceros in africa they shot tigers  from high on elephants until by the  early 20th century few tigers remained  in 1900 the british government hosted  the first international conference on  african wildlife which achieved little  but established precedence for more  productive efforts in the 20th century  all these effects were a product of the  industrial revolution which began on a a  rainy little island with a coupling of  coal burning steam engines to textile  manufacturing  the age of modernity had begun who could  have foreseen the incredible impact it  would have on human lives and on the  environment that sustained our species  [Music]  hey thanks for watching if you'd like to  learn more about the topics in this  episode the full list of series that  these clips came from is in the  description below you can watch them all  on one dream and don't forget to  subscribe to this youtube channel for  new episodes of perspectives and you can  watch previous ones here

